29, 56.599, 56.669, 56.843, 56.924, 57.435, 57.562, 57.644, 57.76, 57.876, 57.969, 58.027, 58.201, 58.375, 58.84]}, "text": "By the fifth century B.C., the Cyclopes have a Sicilian address, next to Mount Etna. Euripides sets a comic play there.\n\nLater, the poet Callimachus sends the smiths to the same neighbourhood, to work for the smith god.\n\nThoo-sididdeez lists the Cyclopes among the earliest people spoken of in Sicily. Then he admits, \"I cannot tell of what race they were.\"\n\nLocals, he records, believed the smith god kept a forge on one of the Aeolian islands. Flame by night. Smoke by day.\n\nForges need fire. Sicily had volcanoes. You can see how the two met.\n\nIn the third century B.C., Thee-ockritus has Pollifeemus sing a love song to a sea nymph. He frets about his looks: one long unbroken eyebrow, and one eye.\n\nPlausible. Provable? Also no."}